      "title": "README - Lipsync Chain",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Lipsync Chain - MiniMax FastH3\n**Geekatplay Studio - Vladimir Chopine**\nhttps://www.geekatplay.com\n\nLoad a video of any length, optionally load a separate dialogue track, and this\nre-generates the whole thing with the mouth driven by the speech.\n\n```\nLoadVideo ---.\n              +--> Lipsync Splitter --> Lipsync Renderer --> SaveVideo\nLoadAudio ---'        (plan)              (per segment)\n```\n\n### Why it splits\nMiniMax H3 cannot render an arbitrary length. Frame counts must sit on the **17k+5**\ngrid (5, 22, 39 ... 124, 141, 158, 175 ...), and a 3-minute video is far past what one\nrender can do. So the timeline is cut into pieces, each piece is re-generated with its\nown slice of the dialogue anchored as an audio guide, and the pieces are joined back up.\n\n### Why the cuts are not evenly spaced\nA cut in the middle of a word is audible. For every segment the splitter tries **all**\ngrid-legal lengths inside `min_seconds`-`max_seconds` and picks the one that lands in a\npause rather than inside a word. With `faster-whisper` installed it uses real word\ntimings; without it, the audio's energy envelope. That is why segments come out 6.58s,\n6.58s, 5.88s, 8.71s and so on instead of all the same length.\n\nThe `split_report` output lists every segment with its time range, the words in it, and\nwhether the cut was **clean**, **ok** or **IN WORD**.\n"
      ]
    },
