      "title": "Reviewing & re-rendering segments",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Reviewing and fixing segments\n\n**Every segment is saved on its own** to\n`output/lipsync/MiniMaxH3_segment_001.mp4`, `_002.mp4`, ... with a small `.json` beside\neach recording how it was made.\n\n`segment_thumbnails` (both nodes) feeds a Preview Image node showing the first frame of\nevery segment, so you can see at a glance which one went wrong.\n\n### Re-rendering one bad segment\nPut its number in the renderer's **`rerender_segments`**:\n\n| you type | what happens |\n|---|---|\n| *(empty)* | everything finished is reused |\n| `3` | segment 3 renders again, the rest is reused |\n| `2, 5-7` | segments 2, 5, 6 and 7 render again |\n| `all` | the whole video renders again |\n\nNothing else changes, so a fix costs one segment's render time, not the whole video.\n\n## Timing is preserved exactly\n- Segments are **disjoint** slices, so no seam frame is trimmed - trimming would push\n  every later word off its frame.\n- `keep_source_audio` is on, so the finished video carries your original dialogue track,\n  not the model's generated audio. The words stay exactly where they were.\n- A source that is not 24 fps is resampled to 24 fps first, so a frame index means the\n  same instant in the source and in the render.\n\n## Speed\nEvery segment is a full FastH3 render. A 3-minute video at ~6.5s per segment is about\n28 segments. Start with a short clip.\n"
      ]
    },
